## `OrreryState`

The whole of Orrery's state. Every field is present from the moment the
world opens, and every one is plain data, so a scenario is posed by writing
the fields posed and read back by reading them.

- `screen`, `mode`, `menuIndex`, `selectIndex`, `howtoPage` — where the
  player is. `selectIndex` is the highlighted select row of the current mode,
  and `howtoPage` the how-to's current page, `0` whenever the screen is not
  `howto`.
- `unlockedCount` — how many campaign challenges are open, `1` at the start.
- `campaignSolved`, `extrasSolved` — solved indices, ascending, no
  duplicates.
- `campaignRecords`, `extrasRecords` — per-challenge records, `null` until a
  challenge's first completion; each metric is the session's best
  independently.
- `campaignMachines`, `extrasMachines` — the per-challenge stashes of
  `specs/editor.md`, `null` for a challenge never opened.
- `campaignLast`, `extrasLast` — the index each mode's select screen lands
  on, `0` until something is entered or solved.
- `challenge`, `challengeRef` — the challenge open in the editor and where
  it came from; `challengeRef` is `null` when the challenge was loaded
  directly through the surface of `specs/instrumentation.md`. Both are
  `null` away from the editor.
- `editor`, `sim` — the machine and the run.
- `pointer` — the pointer as of this frame, in stage units, refreshed by the
  player controller every tick.
- `completion` — whether a boundary that satisfies every set's target
  completes the run, `true` while the game is played.
  `specs/instrumentation.md` states the switch and what holding it off
  leaves the run doing.
- `simTime` — accumulated simulation time, in seconds. Every frame adds its
  `dt`, whatever the screen; the game mode's tick is where it accumulates.
- `muted` — the game's readable copy of the engine's mute bit, refreshed
  every frame. The engine owns muting, and this field is what the debug
  surface reports.

The four per-challenge arrays hold one entry per challenge of their mode,
`campaignRecords` and `campaignMachines` at the course length the build
ships and `extrasRecords` and `extrasMachines` at `EXTRA_COUNT` (`10`).
They are built to those lengths when the world opens, and a `reset` rebuilds
them the same way.

`OrreryState` extends the engine's `GameState`, so an instance also carries
the framework's own `world`, `players`, `phase`, and `elapsed`. Orrery's
screens run on `screen` rather than on the match phase: the mode never calls
`setPhase`, so `phase` stays `"waiting"` and `elapsed` stays `0`, and the
one player the mode adds is `players[0]`, whose controller is where input is
read.

Progress for both modes is carried whatever the mode being played, so the
state has one shape.

## The contract

The declaration above is what the debug surface reads and poses.

- Keep every field under its declared name, type, and meaning.
- The engine builds the state when the world opens, through the mode's
  `gameStateClass`, so every field is present by the time a frame can
  observe it, and none of them is optional.
- The state is the whole of the authoritative game. Every value the game
  carries from one frame to the next lives in it; nothing the simulation, the
  editor, or the screens read is kept in a module-level variable or a
  closure, and any field an actor, component, or controller holds of its own
  is derived data rebuilt from the declared ones. Presentation is the
  exception: the decoded assets, the particle instances now playing, and the
  cues a pose has raised for the next frame to sound, which
  `specs/instrumentation.md` requires a pose to defer, belong to the layer
  that draws and sounds a frame rather than to the state. Nothing the
  snapshot reports is read from there, no rule of the game turns on it, and a
  `reset` clears it.
- The state is written only through the paths play runs on: the player
  controller resolves the frame's input into it, the game mode's tick
  advances it, and the debug surface's poses arrange it at the call.
  Rendering reads it and writes nothing.
- A `reset` restores the declared fields to the initializers above, so those
  fields are the whole of the authoritative state.
{{/if}}
